xv.py	Argument check plus separate FileNotFoundError, PermissionError, and UnicodeDecodeError handlers with clear messages; regex and max() over a generator...nice pythonic-ness. Comments are tight and just enough. Any thoughts on finding strings of integers seperated by comma's, like on line 2896 of the input file: "almost 559,000 of your miles"? 
mj.py	Thirty lines - nice: specific FileNotFoundError and PermissionError handlers, short comments, list comprehension to ints then max(), all nice. Among the tightest self-contained version that read cleanly end-to-end. one minus: UnicodeDecodeError except branch on the open, unlike some others in our class.  Any thoughts on finding strings of integers seperated by comma's, like on line 2896 of the input file: "almost 559,000 of your miles"?  
hw.py	Separate IndexError, FileNotFoundError, and PermissionError with custom error msgs - awesome.  Consider having a UnicodeDecodeError except on file open - as many of the peer reviews I have seen have. Excellent use of imbedding the generator in the print - I appreciate your doing so! Also, I like the thoughtful wrapping the output in a sentence.
5z.py	Consider some basic comments - why does this exist? how is it supposed to be used? what is the re.findall all about? Great:you have the same family of specific except blocks and tailored messages, including UTF-8 decode.  The manual loop for the greatest is easy to read and correct, but long winded.  Perhaps consider using something like this once you test the length of numbers not being zero: a functional style one liner:  greatest = max(map(int, numbers)); or a generator expression one liner: greatest = max(int(x) for x in numbers).  Either removes your for loop. And you could even put it inside a print -like so: print(max(int(x) for x in numbers)), so if that was in an if such as:  if numbers; print(max(int(x) for x in numbers)) and then else: print("No integers found.").  You've have one one line for the for loop and the print statement of the max int! 
5q.py	Preface – I am very impressed with your logic and structure.  There was an exception thrown, and I was just going to say ‘it doesn’t work’, when I looked at your code, and realized, you just needed some more time to get this working and your logic was awesome.  I spent quite some time looking at your code, and so I have more comments than normal, but I learned a ton by reviewing your approach, investigating alternatives, and in the end, like I said, your code was awesome and but only a tiny bit away for the best.  First admin – please add comments, some, at least.  Tell why the program exists, describe any novel things.  Also, please include try / except blocks around file open logic, and catch the explicit exceptions and print helpful comments on how to resolve. (for file opens, have these three at least:  FileNotFoundError, PermissionError and UnicodeDecodeError,).  With your file opened and read into a single string, your for loop iterates through each character of the file or each ‘i’, but you never reference each instance of ‘i’ inside the for loop.  In the for loop code, the re.search(nums, file) does this: it returns the FIRST thing matching the regex pattern (nums).  Every time that re.search gets executed, it returns the FIRST thing – which is a problem I’ll come back to in second.  re.search returns the found thing (the first occurrence of it in the file)…and it puts it inside something called  a Match object.  You assigned it to ‘numbers’.  You have to use numbers.group() or numbers.group(0) to get to the text that re.search found.  You used int(numbers) which tries to change instance of the the Match object to a native data type in python, ‘int’, and the execution stops/exception thrown.  To get what you intended, you should have used int(numbers.group()).  If it then made it past the cast to int, the for loop issue raises its head:  you would have iterated through all the characters in the file, and taken  the FIRST match of the search string (re.search(r"[0-9]+", file) returns the first occurrence) and appended it to your ‘list’.   If the first int in the file is zero (0), you’d get a very long list of zeros.   Instead of re.search, you need to search for ALL the hits in the file, not only the first.  There is a function, re.findall that does just that.  If you use numbers=re,findall(r"[0-9]+", file)... that finds ALL matches, and  returns a List of the strings containing the numbers, which you COULD iterate through.  I loved your intent, and I’d like to show you what your approach did for me – I ended up removing the for, replaced re.search with re.findall, and moved the max to inside your print.  That got you to the shortest program in the batch I reviewed.  Check it out:  4 lines of code after the open stuff (not counting the try excepts that should have been in here, so they don’t count): Line1) num = r"[0-9]+" Line2) numbers = re.findall(num, file) Line3) numbers = [int(i) for i in numbers]  Line4) print(max(numbers)) !!!  Line3 iterates the list of matched strings (each i is one full match like "123") and casts each to int.  I see what you were trying, and honestly I wish I had thought of it.
h9.py	Short digit scanner with just-enough step comments. No try/except for argv nor for open, so bad paths and missing args bubble up. Only concatenates positive digit runs, so the max can differ if a negative integer is the true maximum. But that is likely due to the requirment not detailing what is 'done'.  
z8.py	Comments, please? Very compact, the loop has the only except (and it's a general except) around int() swallows all errors; use ValueError for failed parses, and inlcude catches for file related issues like  FileNotFoundError, PermissionError and UnicodeDecodeError, each with custom error msgs to provide the user some guidance if these common file processing errors pop up.  